-- WRITE-CHANNEL laundering at an ARGUMENT SITE. The REJECT twin of
-- write-workaround-accept, and the reason #2109's fix could not be a
-- location-based special case.
--
-- Slice 4 (S-write-narrowing-direction) measured that the safe narrowing
-- (`Ref noop` into a `<Stdout>` slot) and the genuine #1098-shaped
-- laundering (`idr box`, coercing an ALREADY-TYPED cell) reach
-- `paramVarianceGuard` indistinguishably: the same two `Mono`s, the same
-- rendered `<Stdout> vs <>` pair, and BOTH with the caret on an argument
-- span. "Argument position implies fresh construction" is therefore false,
-- and this row is the falsifier, kept live.
--
-- Here the widening runs in the LAUNDERING direction: `box` is a real cell
-- typed `Ref (String -> <> Unit)`, coerced through the identity `idr` to a
-- type claiming `<Stdout>`. Writing the genuinely effectful `shouty` through
-- `alias` mutates the SAME cell `box` reads from, so `callBox` -- declared
-- with an EMPTY row -- performs `<Stdout>`. EFFECTS-SEMANTICS.md §5 (no
-- laundering) and §9: this MUST be REJECTED, at the same argument site shape
-- where write-workaround-accept must be ACCEPTED.
idr : Ref a -> Ref a
idr r = r

noop : String -> Unit
noop s = ()

shouty : String -> <Stdout> Unit
shouty s =
  let _ = putStr "SMUGGLED "
  ()

box : Ref (String -> <> Unit)
box = Ref noop

alias : Ref (String -> <Stdout> Unit)
alias = idr box

writeIntoAlias : Unit -> Unit
writeIntoAlias u = alias := shouty

callBox : Unit -> Unit
callBox u = box.value "hi"

main =
  let _ = writeIntoAlias ()
  println (callBox ())
